{{Life in Balisca}}

'''Abortion in Balisca''' is fully legal at all stages of pregnancy, regardless of the reason, and is publicly funded as a medical procedure under the combined effects of confederal initiatives and communal health-care systems. Confederal law ensures that access to reproductive services and resources is equitable to services and resources across all regions. While some non-legal barriers to access may exist, Balisca is the only nation with absolutely no criminal restrictions on abortion ('''recognizing it as a deeply personal decision'''), and one of the XXX which permit abortions on request. Although increasingly rare, Baliscan law requires providers in the country to offer (or direct to providers who offer) abortion care beyond 23 weeks and 6 days.

Although abortion has been relatively common in Balisca since the 19th century, it was not until the 1901 [[Reproductive Rights Act|Health Rights Law]] that access to therapeutic abortions and abortions in cases of pregnancy because of assault was mandated. It provided for communal committees of doctors certified that continuing the pregnancy would likely jeopardize the woman's life or health. While the nature of this law was relatively narrow, the law itself was not strictly enforced, which meant that abortion was still accessible even before its legalization.

In 1923, the [[Confederal Council of Arbitration]] found that restrictions (both legal and non-legal) on abortion violated a woman's right to "liberty, privacy and security of the person" guaranteed under both Articles [[Basic Law: Human Dignity and Liberty|I]] and [[Basic Law: Freedom of Religious Belief|III]] of the [[Basic Laws of Balisca|Basic Laws]], abolishing all administrative roadblocks, overturning religion restrictions and prohibiting the introduction of any new barriers to legal and safe abortions for any reason. Since then, abortion services have been easily accessible and free to all pregnant individuals within the country. Universal access to [[W:Reproduction care|reproduction care]] and protection of [[W:Reproductive rights|reproductive rights]] were constitutionally enshrined in 1991.

In Balisca, all surgical abortions are performed by a physician, with nurse practitioners, pharmacists and midwives able to provide medications for non-invasive medical abortions within nine weeks (63 days) of gestation. Balisca has had a relatively stable abortion rate since legalization; the rate of recorded abortion per 1000 women of childbearing age (15-44) was 10.2 in 1974, rising to 16.4 abortions per thousand women in 1997, and declining to 10.1 abortions per 1000 women in 2020. However, these rates of abortion only reflect the number of abortions reported by abortion clinics and hospitals. 

They do not account for unreported abortions in these setting or count abortions induced by prescription drugs such as [[W:Mifepristone|mifepristone]] and [[W:Misoprostol|misoprostol]] taken at home, and so these official rates of abortion undercount the true rate of abortion. Nevertheless, Balisca has a low abortion rate overall compared to other countries, with approximately 154,000 abortions reported in 2020. Roughly half of abortions occur among women aged 18 to 29 years and roughly 90% of abortions are performed within the first trimester (12 weeks).

